---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="Release & setup — Rotagivan"
  description="Follow Rotagivan’s path to a customer-ready Mac release and learn how installation, permissions, and your first workflow will work."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <section class="page-hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">YOUR MAC. YOUR NEXT MOVE.</p>
      <h1>
        Less setup.
        <br />
        <em>More possibility.</em>
      </h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A Mac app should start with a download, not a development environment.
        That’s the experience we’re preparing.
      </p>
    </section>
    <div class="notice">
      <strong>Public download coming soon.</strong>
      <br />
      Rotagivan is in development. A signed, notarized customer release and
      verified system requirements are not available yet. In the meantime, you
      can explore the browser demo.
    </div>
    <section class="setup-grid" aria-label="Planned customer setup">
      <article>
        <span class="step-number">01 / INSTALL</span>
        <h2>Download. Open.</h2>
        <p>
          The planned release will arrive as a Mac download. Move Rotagivan into
          Applications and launch it. No compiler or personal signing
          certificate required.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="step-number">02 / CONNECT</span>
        <h2>Give it permission.</h2>
        <p>
          Allow Accessibility so Rotagivan can run your chosen actions.
          Depending on your input setup, macOS may also ask for Input
          Monitoring. Voice features require microphone permission.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="step-number">03 / MAKE IT YOURS</span>
        <h2>Start with one move.</h2>
        <p>
          Add your favorite app to the HUD, give it a hotkey or gesture, and try
          it. Build up your workspace one useful action at a time.
        </p>
      </article>
    </section>
    <div class="download-state">
      <h2>Feel it before you install it.</h2>
      <p>
        Explore the wheel, tile a demo window, and play a sample workflow.
        Nothing changes on your computer.
      </p>
      <div class="button-row">
        <a class="button" href="/#playground">
          Open the interactive demo
        </a>
        <a class="quiet-link" href="/pricing/">
          View pricing status
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <section class="faq">
      <h2>A little clarity.</h2>
      <details open>
        <summary>Which Macs will it support?</summary>
        <p>
          The app currently targets macOS 13 or later in its bundle metadata.
          The public support range and available processor architectures will be
          published after release testing. Apple trackpad support remains
          experimental; Navigator testing has focused on Voyager.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Why does it need Accessibility?</summary>
        <p>
          Rotagivan uses macOS permissions to send configured inputs and manage
          windows. You control those grants in System Settings → Privacy &
          Security. Signing the app does not grant these permissions
          automatically.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What stays local, and what goes online?</summary>
        <p>
          Keyboard, gesture, and HUD actions run on your Mac. Opening a website
          uses your browser’s network connection. Voice sends audio and command
          data to configured cloud providers, and account sync transfers
          settings when you explicitly save or load them. Those optional
          services have separate setup.
        </p>
      </details>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>
